The '''2026''' '''Newcastle City Council election''' occurred on 7 May 2026,<ref>{{cite web|url=https://new.newcastle.gov.uk/council-elections/elections-and-voting/upcoming-elections|title=Upcoming elections|website=Newcastle City Council|access-date=7 December 2025}}</ref> and all 78 seats on [[Newcastle City Council]] were up for election.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.chroniclelive.co.uk/news/north-east-news/rachel-reeves-spotted-newcastle-labour-32154224|title=Rachel Reeves spotted in Newcastle as Chancellor joins by-election campaign|first=Daniel|last=Holland|date=July 28, 2025|website=Chronicle Live}}</ref> The election will be held alongside [[2026 United Kingdom local elections|other local elections across Great Britain]].

== Background ==
Since the [[2024 Newcastle City Council election|previous council election in 2024]], the council fell into [[no overall control]], with the [[Labour Party (UK)|Labour Party]] remaining the single largest party but losing control of the council. The [[Liberal Democrats (UK, 2025)|Liberal Democrats]] remained the largest opposition.

In August 2025, [[Jamie Driscoll]], former [[Mayor of the North of Tyne]] and former Newcastle City Council member, announced he was standing in the election in [[Monument, Newcastle upon Tyne|Monument ward]]. He said that a progressive alliance of [[Majority (organisation)|Majority]], the [[Green Party of England and Wales|Green Party]], and [[Independent politician|independents]] could win control of Newcastle City Council. In December 2025, Driscoll joined the Green Party.<ref name=":5">{{Cite news |last=Holland |first=Daniel |date=18 August 2025 |title=Ex-mayor Jamie Driscoll to stand for election in Newcastle under 'progressive alliance' plans |url=https://www.chroniclelive.co.uk/news/north-east-news/ex-mayor-jamie-driscoll-stand-32298326 |access-date=7 December 2025 |work=Chronicle Live}}</ref>

